#sleela 3.0
<< pure-gasoline-marker-demo.sleela -- the runnable reference for the syntax 3.0
   `pure` and `gasoline` document markers (SLEELA.syntax section 29).

   The bare top-level words `pure;` and `gasoline;` are a pair of Preonems that
   mean NEARLY THE SAME THING: each claims this document is steered by, and
   stands on, its OWN honesty. `pure` -- clean and unmixed, true to itself;
   `gasoline` -- the same, in the sense of a refined, usable grade. A document
   may carry either or both; the standings compose with the other markers.

   The markers are DECLARATIVE ONLY: siblings to author/authorial (section 18),
   material (section 19), memory (section 20), determine (section 23), and
   object/field (section 24). They change nothing about parsing, typing,
   dispatch, or codegen -- they record standing, not behaviour. Gated to
   #sleela 3.0.

   This demo carries both markers to show they compose. Run it to see it
   compiles exactly as the same document without the markers would:
     sleela run examples/pure-gasoline-marker-demo.sleela >>

pure;
gasoline;

class PureGasolineDocument {
  String standing = "steered by its own honesty";

  void main() {
    print("pure -- clean, unmixed, true to itself");
    print("gasoline -- a refined, usable grade of the same");
    print("the two mean nearly the same thing and compose");
    print("standing: " + standing);
    print("done");
  }
}
